ISSUANCE OF BARANGAY PROTECTION ORDER (BPO)

    A Barangay Protection Order (BPO) is a protection order issued under the Anti-Violence Against Women and Their Children Act (Republic Act No. 9262).

Steps:
    The victim-survivor or petitioner reports the incident to the Barangay and approaches the Officer of the Day for assistance.
    The Officer of the Day refers the victim-survivor or petitioner to the Punong Barangay for an interview and documentation of the incident. The Punong Barangay advises and assists the applicant in filing an application for a Barangay Protection Order.
    The victim-survivor or petitioner submits a written application for a Barangay Protection Order. The application must be signed by the applicant and attested before the Punong Barangay.
    The Punong Barangay conducts an ex-parte proceeding to determine the basis for issuing the Barangay Protection Order.
    After determining the basis for issuance, the Punong Barangay issues the Barangay Protection Order on the date of filing.
    If the Punong Barangay is unavailable, any available Barangay Kagawad may act on the application and issue the Barangay Protection Order. In this case, the Barangay Kagawad must include an attestation stating that the Punong Barangay was unavailable at the time of issuance.
    Immediately after issuance, the Punong Barangay or Barangay Kagawad personally serves a copy of the Barangay Protection Order to the respondent or directs another barangay official to serve it.
    Important Information:
    The Barangay Protection Order is effective for fifteen (15) days.
    The issuance of a BPO follows an ex-parte proceeding.
    The respondent must be served with a copy of the issued BPO immediately.
Legal Basis:
    Republic Act No. 9262, Section 14 – Barangay Protection Orders (BPOs); Who May Issue and How.